**Read:** [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), molecular conductance; [Fundamentals of Vacuum Physics and Technology — Eshraq Al-Dmour](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Physics-Al-Dmour.lingua#r_175RE9FCKASP84H5VPDDPDX0C9), tubes and effective pumping. Preserve the diameter, radius and length conventions explicitly.

For a long circular tube with diffuse wall reflection in molecular flow, a useful asymptotic result is C≈πd³v̄/(12L). It is a long-tube limit, not a formula for every aspect ratio. The exact short-passage result must approach the aperture limit as thickness tends to zero rather than diverge.

The molecular transmission probability depends on geometry and scattering. Elbows, baffles, valves and inserts can alter it substantially. A large nominal port connected through a restrictive internal passage may deliver much less effective pumping than the port diameter suggests.

**Work:** Derive the long-tube scaling with diameter, length, temperature and molar mass. Compare doubling diameter with doubling pumping speed for a conductance-limited chamber. Test the formula against the aperture upper bound and identify where the asymptotic expression becomes inadmissible. Separate uncertain geometry from uncertain wall-scattering behavior.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
**Continue in gas and vacuum systems:** [Viscous gas flow and pressure-dependent conductance](Viscous-gas-flow-and-pressure-dependent-conductance.lingua#r_7MT7K55E3BZFY7JE54FZVV32Y9).

